from lib import *
D="d4"

L("4-01",D,"d4c1","DHCP and DNS",["4.3","4.6"],35,
"DHCP automatically gives devices their IP address, mask, default gateway, and DNS servers. DNS translates human-friendly names like www.example.com into IP addresses. Together they make networks usable without manual configuration.",
[(B,"DHCP DORA","Discover (client broadcast looking for servers), Offer (server proposes an address), Request (client asks for the offered address, broadcast), Acknowledge (server confirms). Clients use UDP 68, servers UDP 67. Leases expire and are renewed."),
 (I,"Cisco IOS DHCP server, relay and client","ip dhcp excluded-address 192.168.10.1 192.168.10.10 / ip dhcp pool LAN10 / network 192.168.10.0 255.255.255.0 / default-router 192.168.10.1 / dns-server 8.8.8.8 / lease 7. Because routers do not forward broadcasts, when the server is on another subnet configure a relay on the client-facing gateway interface: 'ip helper-address 10.0.0.50'. The relay converts the broadcast into a unicast to the server and fills in the giaddr field so the server picks the right pool. A router interface can be a DHCP client: 'ip address dhcp'. Verify with 'show ip dhcp binding', 'show ip dhcp pool', 'show ip dhcp conflict'."),
 (X,"DNS","DNS uses UDP 53 for standard queries and TCP 53 for zone transfers and large responses. Record types: A (IPv4), AAAA (IPv6), CNAME (alias), MX (mail), PTR (reverse lookup), NS (name server). A client asks its configured resolver; the resolver may query root, TLD, and authoritative servers recursively. On IOS: 'ip name-server 8.8.8.8', 'ip domain-lookup' (default on), 'ip domain-name example.com'; many admins use 'no ip domain-lookup' in labs to stop mistyped commands from being resolved as hostnames.")],
"Almost every endpoint relies on DHCP and DNS. When they fail, users experience 'the internet is down' even though routing works.",
[("Central DHCP","Branch router Gi0/1 (VLAN 10 gateway) has 'ip helper-address 10.100.1.5' to reach the HQ DHCP server."),
 ("Name resolution failure","ping 8.8.8.8 works but ping www.cisco.com fails → check DNS server settings.")],
["Placing 'ip helper-address' on the server-facing interface instead of the client-facing interface.",
 "Forgetting to exclude gateway and server addresses from the pool.",
 "Thinking DNS uses only TCP or only UDP."],
[("DORA","Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge."),
 ("DHCP relay","Gateway function forwarding DHCP broadcasts as unicasts (ip helper-address)."),
 ("Lease","Time a client may use an assigned address."),
 ("A / AAAA record","DNS mapping of a name to IPv4 / IPv6 address.")],
["DHCP: server UDP 67, client UDP 68; DORA.",
 "ip helper-address goes on the interface facing the clients.",
 "DNS: UDP/TCP 53; A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, PTR, NS.",
 "'ip address dhcp' makes an IOS interface a DHCP client."],
["Know which DHCP messages are broadcast vs unicast in the basic flow (Discover and Request are broadcast from the client)."],
"DHCP assigns IP settings through DORA, with relays forwarding requests across subnets; DNS resolves names to addresses over port 53.",
[("Where is 'ip helper-address' configured?",["On the DHCP server","On the interface receiving client broadcasts","On every switch port","In global configuration"],1,"On the client-facing gateway interface."),
 ("Which DHCP message does the server send first?",["Discover","Offer","Request","Ack"],1,"Server responds to Discover with an Offer."),
 ("Which DNS record maps a name to an IPv6 address?",["A","AAAA","PTR","MX"],1,"AAAA records hold IPv6 addresses.")],
diagrams=["dhcp_dora"])

L("4-02",D,"d4c1","NAT and PAT",["4.1","1.7"],40,
"NAT translates private inside addresses to public addresses as traffic leaves the network. PAT (NAT overload) lets thousands of inside devices share a single public IP by tracking each connection's port number.",
[(B,"Why NAT exists","Public IPv4 addresses are scarce. Organizations use private RFC 1918 addresses internally and translate them at the internet edge."),
 (I,"The four address terms","Inside local: the inside host's real (usually private) address. Inside global: the public address representing the inside host. Outside local: the outside host's address as seen from inside (usually same as outside global). Outside global: the outside host's real public address. Mark interfaces with 'ip nat inside' and 'ip nat outside'."),
 (X,"Static NAT, dynamic NAT, and PAT configuration","Static (one-to-one, permanent, allows inbound connections): ip nat inside source static 192.168.1.10 203.0.113.10. Dynamic (pool, one-to-one while in use): access-list 1 permit 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 / ip nat pool PUB 203.0.113.20 203.0.113.30 netmask 255.255.255.0 / ip nat inside source list 1 pool PUB. If the pool is exhausted, new hosts fail. PAT: ip nat inside source list 1 interface GigabitEthernet0/1 overload (or 'pool PUB overload'). Verify with 'show ip nat translations' and 'show ip nat statistics'; clear dynamic entries with 'clear ip nat translation *'. Debug with 'debug ip nat'.")],
"Nearly every internet-connected network uses PAT. Understanding NAT terms is essential for reading translation tables and troubleshooting.",
[("Home/office PAT","192.168.1.10:51000 and 192.168.1.11:51000 both leave as 203.0.113.2, with PAT assigning unique source ports (e.g., 51000 and 51001) to keep them apart."),
 ("Published server","Static NAT maps web server 192.168.1.10 to 203.0.113.10 so internet users can reach it.")],
["Reversing 'ip nat inside' and 'ip nat outside' on interfaces.",
 "ACL used for NAT not matching the inside subnet.",
 "Confusing inside local with inside global.",
 "Forgetting 'overload', which turns PAT into dynamic NAT and quickly exhausts addresses."],
[("Inside local","Inside host's private address."),
 ("Inside global","Public address representing the inside host."),
 ("PAT","Port Address Translation (NAT overload)."),
 ("Translation table","Mapping of inside/outside addresses and ports.")],
["Inside local → inside global on the way out.",
 "Static = 1:1 permanent; dynamic = pool; PAT = many-to-one with ports ('overload').",
 "show ip nat translations; show ip nat statistics."],
["v1.1 objective 4.1: configure and verify inside source NAT using static and pools; PAT is commonly tested too.",
 "Read a 'show ip nat translations' table and identify each column."],
"NAT translates inside local to inside global addresses. Use static NAT for servers, dynamic NAT with pools, and PAT (overload) to share one public address among many hosts.",
[("In NAT terminology, the private IP of an internal PC is the:",["Inside global","Inside local","Outside local","Outside global"],1,"Inside local is the internal host's real address."),
 ("Which keyword enables PAT?",["pat","overload","extendable","many"],1,"'overload'."),
 ("Which command shows active translations?",["show nat","show ip nat translations","show ip nat pool","debug nat"],1,"show ip nat translations.")],
diagrams=["nat_pat"])

L("4-03",D,"d4c2","NTP, SNMP, and Syslog",["4.2","4.4","4.5"],35,
"Network management relies on three services: NTP keeps every device's clock accurate, SNMP lets monitoring systems read and change device information, and Syslog sends event messages to a central server. Accurate time makes logs meaningful.",
[(B,"NTP","NTP synchronizes clocks over UDP 123. Stratum indicates distance from an authoritative source: stratum 0 is a reference clock (GPS/atomic), stratum 1 servers connect to it, and so on (lower is more accurate). Configure a client: 'ntp server 192.0.2.1'. Make a router an NTP server: 'ntp master 4' (or simply have it synchronized and serving). Verify: 'show ntp status', 'show ntp associations'; set display time zone with 'clock timezone'."),
 (I,"SNMP","An SNMP manager (NMS) polls agents on devices using UDP 161; agents send unsolicited Traps or Informs (acknowledged) to the manager on UDP 162. Data is organized in the MIB and identified by OIDs. Operations: Get, GetNext, GetBulk (v2c+), Set, Trap, Inform. SNMPv1/v2c use community strings sent in clear text (read-only/read-write). SNMPv3 adds users with security levels noAuthNoPriv, authNoPriv, and authPriv (authentication and encryption)."),
 (X,"Syslog","Messages look like '%LINEPROTO-5-UPDOWN: Line protocol on Interface Gi0/1, changed state to down' — facility (LINEPROTO), severity (5), mnemonic (UPDOWN), description. Severities: 0 Emergency, 1 Alert, 2 Critical, 3 Error, 4 Warning, 5 Notification, 6 Informational, 7 Debugging (mnemonic: 'Every Awesome Cisco Engineer Will Need Ice cream Daily'). A configured level includes that level and all more severe (lower numbers). Destinations: console ('logging console'), buffer ('logging buffered'), terminal lines ('terminal monitor' for VTY), and servers ('logging host 192.0.2.50', 'logging trap warnings'). Syslog uses UDP 514. Add timestamps with 'service timestamps log datetime msec'.")],
"Without accurate time and centralized logs, you cannot correlate events across devices, investigate incidents, or meet audit requirements.",
[("Logging level","'logging trap 4' sends levels 0-4 (Emergency to Warning) to the syslog server; Notification (5) messages are not sent."),
 ("SNMP monitoring","An NMS polls interface counters every 5 minutes via SNMP Get and receives a Trap when a link goes down.")],
["Thinking 'logging trap 4' sends level 4 and higher numbers; it sends 4 and lower numbers (more severe).",
 "Confusing SNMP agent port 161 with trap port 162.",
 "Expecting SNMPv2c to encrypt community strings.",
 "Forgetting that 'terminal monitor' is needed to see logs in an SSH session."],
[("Stratum","NTP distance from the reference clock."),
 ("MIB / OID","SNMP Management Information Base / Object Identifier."),
 ("Trap / Inform","Unsolicited SNMP notification; Inform is acknowledged."),
 ("Facility","Syslog source category, e.g., LINK, OSPF, SYS.")],
["NTP UDP 123; SNMP UDP 161 (polls) and 162 (traps/informs); Syslog UDP 514.",
 "Syslog levels 0-7: Emergency, Alert, Critical, Error, Warning, Notification, Informational, Debugging.",
 "SNMPv3 authPriv = authentication + encryption.",
 "Logging a level includes all more severe levels."],
["Expect to decode a syslog message and identify its severity level and meaning.",
 "Know SNMP message types and which direction they flow."],
"NTP keeps clocks synchronized by stratum, SNMP polls (161) and receives traps (162) using MIBs/OIDs with v3 adding security, and Syslog sends graded messages (0-7) to local and remote destinations.",
[("Which syslog severity level is 'Warning'?",["2","3","4","5"],2,"4 = Warning."),
 ("Which SNMP message is unsolicited and acknowledged?",["Trap","Inform","Get","Set"],1,"Inform requires an acknowledgment."),
 ("Which port do NTP messages use?",["UDP 123","TCP 123","UDP 161","UDP 514"],0,"NTP = UDP 123.")])

L("4-04",D,"d4c2","Quality of Service (QoS)",["4.7"],35,
"QoS decides which traffic goes first when a link is congested. Voice and video are sensitive to delay, so they get priority, while bulk downloads can wait. QoS does not create bandwidth; it manages it.",
[(B,"What hurts real-time traffic","Bandwidth (capacity), delay/latency (time to arrive), jitter (variation in delay), and loss (dropped packets). Cisco guidance for voice: one-way delay ≤ 150 ms, jitter ≤ 30 ms, loss ≤ 1%."),
 (I,"Classification and marking","Classification identifies traffic (by ACL, NBAR, or existing markings). Marking writes a value into the header so other devices can act on it: Layer 2 CoS (3 bits in the 802.1Q PCP field, 0-7); Layer 3 DSCP (6 bits in the IP header's ToS/Traffic Class byte, 0-63). Common DSCP values: EF (46) for voice, AF classes (AF41 for video, etc.), CS values. A trust boundary is where markings start being trusted, ideally at the IP phone or access switch."),
 (X,"Queuing, congestion avoidance, policing and shaping","Queuing schedules packets when an interface is congested: CBWFQ guarantees bandwidth per class; LLQ adds a strict priority queue for voice (with a policer to prevent starvation of other queues). Congestion avoidance: tail drop discards new packets when the queue is full, causing TCP global synchronization; WRED randomly drops lower-priority packets earlier to avoid that. Policing enforces a rate by dropping or re-marking excess traffic (no buffering; ingress or egress, common at provider edges). Shaping buffers excess traffic and sends it later (egress only, adds delay, common on the customer side to match a provider's policed rate).")],
"Converged networks carry voice, video, and data on one infrastructure. Without QoS, a large download can make a phone call unusable.",
[("Branch WAN","Voice marked EF is placed in an LLQ priority queue; business apps get CBWFQ guarantees; everything else is best effort."),
 ("Provider contract","A 1 Gbps circuit is policed by the ISP to 200 Mbps; the customer shapes outbound traffic to 200 Mbps to avoid drops.")],
["Thinking QoS adds bandwidth.",
 "Confusing policing (drop/re-mark, no delay) with shaping (buffer, adds delay).",
 "Mixing up CoS (Layer 2, 3 bits) and DSCP (Layer 3, 6 bits)."],
[("DSCP","Differentiated Services Code Point, 6-bit Layer 3 marking."),
 ("CoS","Class of Service, 3-bit Layer 2 marking in 802.1Q."),
 ("LLQ","Low Latency Queuing: strict priority queue plus CBWFQ."),
 ("WRED","Weighted Random Early Detection congestion avoidance."),
 ("Trust boundary","Point where QoS markings are accepted as valid.")],
["Voice: delay ≤150 ms, jitter ≤30 ms, loss ≤1%; EF = DSCP 46.",
 "Policing drops/re-marks; shaping buffers (egress only).",
 "LLQ = priority queue for voice; WRED avoids TCP global synchronization."],
["v1.1 objective 4.7 is 'Explain the forwarding per-hop behavior (PHB) for QoS'. Focus on concepts, not MQC configuration."],
"QoS classifies and marks traffic (CoS/DSCP), queues it (CBWFQ/LLQ), avoids congestion (WRED), and controls rates (policing vs shaping) to protect delay-sensitive applications.",
[("Which DSCP value is recommended for voice?",["AF41","CS1","EF (46)","0"],2,"EF/DSCP 46."),
 ("Which mechanism buffers excess traffic rather than dropping it?",["Policing","Shaping","WRED","Marking"],1,"Shaping delays excess traffic."),
 ("Which field holds the Layer 2 CoS marking?",["IP ToS byte","802.1Q PCP","TCP flags","EtherType"],1,"CoS is the 3-bit PCP field in the 802.1Q tag.")],
diagrams=["qos"])
